---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../../components/CaseHeader.astro';
import CaseSections from '../../components/CaseSections.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import MarginNote from '../../components/MarginNote.astro';
import EditView from '../../components/EditView.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import Compare from '../../components/Compare.astro';
import Persona from '../../components/Persona.astro';
import Pager from '../../components/Pager.astro';
import { img } from '../../data/images';

const project = (await getEntry('projects', 'sunnyvale-cleanwater'))!;
const sv = (f: string) => img('sunnyvale-cleanwater', f);

const drafts = [
  { file: 'sunnyvalecleanwater_18.png', label: 'Draft 1', alt: 'Header subtitle draft: "Providing reliable service for residents and businesses 24/7".', why: 'Straightforward, but it never mentions water or the environment, and “24/7” felt out of place.' },
  { file: 'sunnyvalecleanwater_19.png', label: 'Draft 2', alt: 'Header subtitle draft: "Ensuring that wastewater is not wasted".', why: 'Wittier, but the wordplay undercut the serious tone, and it narrowed the program to water alone.' },
  { file: 'sunnyvalecleanwater_20.png', label: 'Draft 3', alt: 'Header subtitle draft: "Meeting Service and Environment Goals now and for the future".', why: 'The right message. Three small edits made it the final line.' },
  { file: 'sunnyvalecleanwater_21.png', label: 'Selected', alt: 'Final header subtitle: "Meeting service and environment goals today and for our future".', why: 'The line that was chosen.' },
];
---
<Base title={project.data.title} description={project.data.summary} current="work">
  <article class="wrap">
    <CaseHeader project={project} />
    <CaseSections>

    <Section id="context" title="Context">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          The Sunnyvale Cleanwater Program is a group of projects rebuilding the infrastructure that cleans the city’s wastewater
          for safe public use, mainly the treatment plant itself. A program website already existed, but it was hard to use and
          it wasn’t raising awareness the way the program needed.
        </p>
        <p>
          The new site had two audiences. Residents needed to understand what Sunnyvale was doing with its wastewater.
          Professionals needed to review technical documents and reach the program about contract work.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MarginNote label="Before launch">I worked on the site before it launched. Some content on the live site has changed since.</MarginNote>
    </Section>

    <Section id="team" title="Team">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          I was the UX designer and UX writer. Erin, a water scientist, was the subject matter expert. Elizabeth, the program manager,
          was also the program’s contact with the community.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="personas" title="Personas">
      <div class="col-wide two-up">
        <Persona
          name="Sierra"
          age={32}
          status="Married"
          occupation="Computer engineer"
          location="Sunnyvale, California"
          quote="With so much water around, where does the water I drink come from?"
          bio="Works for a well-known tech company in the area. Curious, and keeps up with what’s happening in her community for her kids."
          needs="Updates on water quality, and how the water she might drink is monitored."
          pains="Doesn’t know where to start finding out about her water, or who to ask."
        />
        <Persona
          name="Tyler"
          age={30}
          status="Single"
          occupation="Water scientist"
          location="Santa Clara, California"
          quote="There’s a lot of commotion in the Sunnyvale construction sites. I wish there was somewhere I can read data that’s readily available."
          bio="A scientist who wants to learn more every day. Watches how nearby cities handle their water and their water studies."
          needs="Research on better water purification, ideally public-facing data he can read without asking."
          pains="Many sites have archaic navigation and poorly organized documents, if they’re organized at all."
        />
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="microcopy" title="Microcopy" kicker="Writing">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          Microcopy across the site needed rewriting. Because it carried no scientific claims, only orientation for visitors,
          it was left to me.
        </p>
        <h3>The header subtitle</h3>
        <p>The line under the site header had to catch the eye and still sound serious. It went through three drafts.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="col-wide drafts">
        {drafts.map((d) => (
          <li class:list={['draft', { 'draft--final': d.label === 'Selected' }]}>
            <p class="meta draft__label">{d.label}</p>
            <Figure src={sv(d.file)} alt={d.alt} size="wide" frame={false} zoom={false} />
            <p class="draft__why">{d.why}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <EditView
        parts={[
          { t: 'Meeting ' },
          { del: 'Service and Environment Goals' },
          { ins: 'service and environment goals' },
          { t: ' ' },
          { del: 'now' },
          { ins: 'today' },
          { t: ' and for ' },
          { del: 'the' },
          { ins: 'our' },
          { t: ' future' },
        ]}
        caption="Draft 3 → selected"
      />
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sentence case</strong> instead of title case.</li>
          <li><strong>“today” for “now”</strong>: the projects play out over days, not moments.</li>
          <li><strong>“our” for “the”</strong>: the future in question is the community’s.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Buttons</h3>
        <p>
          Buttons follow one pattern across the site. An arrow shows how the link behaves. Labels are three words at most,
          with two exceptions: a call to action that has to say where it leads (like the newsletter sign-up), and a button that
          names the file it’s about to download.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure src={sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_11.png')} alt='Overview teaser with a purple "Learn More →" button beside a photo of the plant.' caption="Two words and an arrow." size="margin" />
    </Section>

    <Section id="ia" title="Information architecture" kicker="Design">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          The original site had few pages, each holding many sections. The redesign has more pages, each with less, more specific
          information.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Compare
        a={{ src: sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_5.png'), label: 'Existing site', alt: 'Site map of the existing site: Home branches to Why the Community Needs This Program, About the Existing Plant (with nine sub-pages), Water Pollution Control Master Plan, Program Environmental Impact Report, News and Updates, and Projects.' }}
        b={{ src: sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_6.png'), label: 'Redesign', alt: 'Site map of the new site: Home branches to Learn More, Get Involved, Program Info, Updates, Contact Us and Contractors, each with focused sub-pages.' }}
        caption="Site maps before and after. The new top level is organized by what visitors came to do."
      />
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          The Projects page shows the change best. It used to be one page with a column listing every project, its description
          and its status. Now it links to a page for each project, and each project page links back to the list.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure src={sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_9.png')} alt="Active Projects page: a list of five project links beside a photo of equipment on a construction site." size="text" />
    </Section>

    <Section id="navigation" title="Navigation">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          Testing changed some parts of the site to match what people looked for most. Many visitors wanted to reach the program and
          its manager more than they wanted updates. After A/B testing, Contact Us moved above Updates in the Get Involved menu.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Compare
        a={{ src: sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_15.png'), label: 'Before', alt: 'Navigation menu: under Get Involved, Updates is listed above Contact Us.' }}
        b={{ src: sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_8.png'), label: 'After', alt: 'Navigation menu: under Get Involved, Contact Us is listed above Updates.' }}
      />
    </Section>

    <Section id="layout" title="Content layout">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <h3>FAQ</h3>
        <p>
          The FAQ page was new, and its layout improved over iterations. It started as a plain list of questions and answers.
          The final version opens with “On this page,” a list of the questions linked to their answers. Each answer has a
          “Back to Questions” link, and longer answers sit beside an image so they read in smaller pieces.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Compare
        a={{ src: sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_3.png'), label: 'Early', alt: 'Early FAQ page: questions as headings, each followed by a paragraph of answer text.' }}
        b={{ src: sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_4.png'), label: 'Final', alt: 'Final FAQ page: an "On this page" list of linked questions beside a photo of a scientist.' }}
      />
      <Figure src={sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_10.png')} alt='A long FAQ answer set beside a photo of a pipe labeled "Digested sludge", with a "Back to Questions" link.' caption="A long answer broken up by an image, with a way back to the list." size="text" />
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <h3>Master Plan</h3>
        <p>
          The Master Plan page existed on the old site, but even a list of links could improve. The new layout uses two columns to
          make use of the extra space. Section titles are in the body text color, so nobody mistakes them for links. Large files show
          their size in parentheses, unlinked, next to the link.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Compare
        a={{ src: sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_1.png'), label: 'Original', alt: 'Original Master Plan page: one long single column of document links in blue.' }}
        b={{ src: sv('sunnyvalecleanwater_2.png'), label: 'Redesign', alt: 'Redesigned Master Plan page: documents in two columns under dark section titles, with file sizes such as 47.4 MB after large files.' }}
      />
    </Section>

    <Section id="process" title="Process simplification">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          Some of the plant’s work is complicated: power generation, biosolids production and water recycling on the Benefits, Value,
          Community page, and the program schedule process on the Projects page.
        </p>
        <p>
          I explained each one with icons, arrows and plain language. The icons stand for the nouns in the text that a visitor would
          recognize. Each arrow carries a short phrase for the action that moves the process forward. Water recycling also gets a
          two-sentence summary. The subject matter experts approved both the text and the imagery.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="retrospective" title="Retrospective">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          The best part was learning a subject that was new to me alongside a subject matter expert: rounds of understanding how
          the plant works, then designing information for Sunnyvale residents from that understanding. I also enjoyed organizing
          the content so the site was informative and pleasant at the same time.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I’d do differently: get involved in social media. The site raised awareness and residents complimented it, but there
          was room for more outreach. The City controls its social media; creating content for those channels would have let people
          meet the plant on the apps they already use.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>
    </CaseSections>
  </article>
  <Pager id={project.id} />
</Base>

<style>
  .drafts { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: var(--space-3) 0 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); }
  .draft { display: grid; gap: var(--space-1) var(--gutter); align-items: start; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: var(--space-3); }
  @media (min-width: 760px) { .draft { grid-template-columns: 6rem minmax(0, 1.4fr) minmax(0, 1fr); } }
  .draft :global(.figure) { margin: 0; }
  .draft__label { margin: 0; }
  .draft--final .draft__label { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 700; }
  .draft__why { margin: 0; font-size: var(--step--1); }
</style>
